== The Milnor exact sequence <sec:milnor-sequence>

#idx("Milnor", "exact sequence", target: [@sec:milnor-sequence])#idx(
  "exact",
  "sequence",
  "Milnor",
  target: [@sec:milnor-sequence],
)The *$n$-skeleton*#idx("skeleton") of a simplicial set is the simplicial subset
generated by the $i$-simplices for $i <= n$. When studying homotopy classes of
maps $X -> Y$ from one simplicial set to another, it is often easy to see what
is going on on the $n$-skeleton of $X$ for each $n$, and one would like to pass
to the limit. #source(40)However, it is not true in general that if
$X_0 <= X_1 <= dots.c$ is a sequence of inclusions with $X = union.big X_n$ then
$[X; Y] = invlim [X_n; Y]$. There is also a $attach(invlim, tr: 1)$#idx(
  "$attach(limits(lim), b: arrow.l.long, tr: 1)$",
) term, which we now describe. In Chapter~4 of Volume~I, we described
$attach(invlim, tr: 1)$ for an inverse system of abelian groups; we now need
this notion for inverse systems of groups in general. If
$
  dots.c -> G_2 limits(arrow.r.long)^(alpha_2) G_1
  limits(arrow.r.long)^(alpha_1) G_0
$
are groups and homomorphisms, then the group $product G_n$ acts on the set
$product G_n$ via
$
  (g_0, g_1, dots)(x_0, x_1, dots)
  = (g_0 x_0 alpha_1 (g_1)^(-1), g_1 x_1 alpha_2 (g_2)^(-1), dots)
$
and $attach(invlim, tr: 1) G_n$ is the quotient of the set $product G_n$ by the
equivalence relation defined by this action. It is only a pointed set, and not a
group in general, but if the $G_n$ are abelian then $attach(invlim, tr: 1) G_n$
is an abelian group. An exact sequence
$ {1_n} -> {G'_n} -> {G_n} -> {G''_n} -> {1_n} $
of inverse systems of groups gives rise to a six term exact sequence of groups
and pointed sets
$
  1 -> invlim G'_n -> invlim G_n -> invlim G''_n
  -> attach(invlim, tr: 1) G'_n -> attach(invlim, tr: 1) G_n
  -> attach(invlim, tr: 1) G''_n -> 1.
$

#theorem(
  title: [Milnor; Bousfield--Kan [@bib:Bousfield1972] Chapter~IX §3],
  closed: true,
  head-break: true,
)[
  Suppose that $Y$ is a Kan complex and $X_0 <= X_1 <= dots.c$ is a sequence of
  inclusions of simplicial sets with union $X$. Then there is an exact sequence
  of pointed sets
  $
    1 -> attach(invlim, tr: 1) [S X_n; Y] -> [X; Y] -> invlim [X_n; Y] -> 1.
  $
] <th:milnor-exact-sequence>
